# Saving a genuinely binary response (an image here) to disk, byte-perfect,
# via a "# @download" annotation -- a feature httpfly.nvim's own bundled
# httpyac plugin (httpyac-plugin/httpfly.js) implements, not httpyac itself.
#
# 8_save_response.http's post-request script approach (require("fs") +
# response.body) doesn't work for this: httpyac decodes response.body to a
# JS string somewhere in its own pipeline before any "> {% ... %}" script
# ever sees it, and that's lossy for arbitrary binary bytes -- confirmed by
# testing it against this exact endpoint and finding the saved file's
# first byte, and its overall size, no longer matched the original PNG.
#
# response.rawBody (a real, undecoded Buffer) does exist -- just not by the
# time an inline script runs. It's still there one step earlier, in the
# onResponse hook, which is the only place a plugin (rather than a script
# you write yourself) can get at it -- see httpyac-plugin/httpfly.js.
#
# Saved under .httpfly/downloads/ in your current working directory (same
# place .httpfly/history/ and .httpfly/session.json already live -- one
# .gitignore entry covers all of it). "@download" alone picks a filename
# automatically, in the same priority order browsers use: Content-
# Disposition's filename, then the URL's last path segment if it looks
# like a real filename, then a content-type-guessed extension. httpbin's
# /image/png sends neither of the first two, so DownloadAuto always lands
# on the fallback in practice -- that's still the right thing to have,
# since plenty of real APIs don't send a filename hint either.
# "@download <name>" (see DownloadNamed below) overrides it explicitly.
#
# Run `make httpbin-up` from the repo root first (requires Docker).

###
# @name DownloadAuto
# @download
GET http://localhost:8080/image/png HTTP/1.1

###
# @name DownloadNamed
# @download my-photo.png
GET http://localhost:8080/image/png HTTP/1.1
